Structure of arrays as a switch in odb, demonstrated on iterm_tbl.

NOT UPSTREAMED. Carried against the pinned OpenROAD; retires when a //:bump
lands on an upstream that carries the change.

A table stores its fields as columns rather than as members of its slots.
Two independent switches, one line each in the slot type:

    static constexpr bool kSoaTable = true;         // columns in memory
    static constexpr bool kFieldMajorTable = true;  // columns on the wire

iterm_tbl takes both, and is the only table that does. Nothing else in odb
changes behaviour, which is the point of the shape: whether a table is
columnar is a property of that table, decided in one line, and the two
halves are independent of each other.

## In memory

A slot keeps only what dbObject needs -- an address the public API can hand
out, and the offset word that finds its page. Fields move to columns owned
by the page, reached through accessors that return references, so the
migration is a rename: `iterm->net_` becomes `iterm->net()`, and an
assignment reads as it did. 125 sites, seven files, all inside
src/odb/src/db. Nothing outside odb changes: a dbITerm* is still the slot's
address, so every tool compiles untouched.

Access points are keyed by slot rather than given a column: before pin
access no iterm has one, and a dense column spends 24 bytes a slot on an
empty map.

Peak RSS of read_db, one table converted:

    nangate45/ariane133      274.2 MB -> 243.1 MB   -11.3%
    nangate45/black_parrot   360.9 MB -> 318.2 MB   -11.8%
    sky130hd/microwatt       248.2 MB -> 226.4 MB    -8.8%

Read and write time are unchanged.

## On the wire

An opted-in table writes its pages in blocks whose bytes are permuted
field-major, produced by the type's existing operator<< -- no per-type
serializer, which was the condition the idea was conditional on. A block
spans several pages, so no object id moves; raising a table's page_size
would have the same effect on adjacency and must not be used, because an
id is `page_addr | slot`. Records of different lengths inside a block are
split by length; past 64 distinct lengths the block is written verbatim.

Compressed .odb, one table converted, five public ORFS designs:

    asap7/aes (floorplan)    1.35x        sky130hd/microwatt      1.40x
    asap7/aes (routed)       1.07x        nangate45/black_parrot  1.44x
    nangate45/ariane133      1.41x        aggregate               1.38x

Every design passes two gates: the DEF written after a round trip is
byte-identical to the DEF before it, and write-read-write reproduces the
same bytes. kSchemaFieldMajorBlocks gates the read; older files take the
per-slot path unchanged.

## What this deliberately does not do

Everything below is a further gain the switch makes available, measured
where a number is quoted, and left out so that this change is one concern:

  * Other tables. box_tbl and sbox_tbl on the wire take the aggregate from
    1.38x to 1.53x; every table takes it to 2.51x, but the block buffer is
    sized by sizeof(T) and so does not bind for records carrying a heap
    payload -- all tables costs 7.1x read time and 2.3x peak RSS until
    blocks are bounded at slot granularity rather than page granularity.
  * Columns for other tables in memory. iterm_tbl is the largest table in
    a placed design; box_tbl and sbox_tbl are the next two.
  * oid_ is stored per slot though it is derivable from the slot index --
    another 4 bytes a slot, across every table. It is also the field
    behind #9960, where a stale cached oid on a reused slot reads as
    uninitialized memory.
  * prev_net_iterm_ is derivable from the next_ chain.
  * A sparse column costs nothing for absent entries but its mutable
    accessor creates them, so a read path that asks for a mutable
    reference re-densifies it. Worth a checked accessor if more columns
    become sparse.

